    20	
    21	(outliers)=
    22	
    23	+++
    24	
    25	# Trimming and winsorization transition guide
    26	
    27	An outlier is an observation that differs substantially from other observations in a sample. There are many references available about identifying outliers, whether to make adjustments for outliers, and if so, what adjustments to make. This guide does not make any recommendations on these topics. Instead, the focus is on how to perform two of the most common procedures:
    28	
    29	- trimming: removing outliers from the dataset, and
    30	- winsorization: replacing outliers with a more central value,
    31	
    32	using tools available in SciPy, with an emphasis on transitioning from use of legacy functions to the preferred approach.
    33	
    34	## Motivation
    35	In the past, SciPy has offered several "convenience functions" that combine trimming with computation of a statistic. Consider, for instance, {func}`scipy.stats.trim_mean`.
    36	
    37	```{code-cell} ipython3
    38	import os
    39	os.environ['SCIPY_ARRAY_API'] = '1'
    40	import numpy as np
    41	from scipy import stats
    42	np.set_printoptions(linewidth=120)
    43	x = np.arange(20.)
    44	stats.trim_mean(x, proportiontocut=0.1)
    45	```
    46	
    47	Without carefully reading the documentation, it is not immediately obvious how the operation was performed: does `proportiontocut` refer to the proportion to cut from each tail, or is it the total proportion of the data to remove?
    48	
    49	Likewise, when the proportion multiplied by the number of elements would not result in an integer, is the number to remove rounded up or down?
    50	
    51	What if instead of removing a proportion, we want to remove data below and above specified thresholds. It turns out that we cannot achieve this with {func}`trim_mean <scipy.stats.trim_mean>`; we would need to use {func}`tmean <scipy.stats.tmean>` or (the similarly-named, but curiously relegated to a separate namespace) {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.trimmed_mean`, instead.
    52	
    53	What if we want to remove data outside a "confidence interval" estimate. Do any of these functions offer anything to facilitate that?  No, not really.
    54	
    55	One could take the mean after using one of the seven functions for trimming data. But which one(s) will do what we need?
    56	
    57	- {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.trim`
    58	- {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.trima`
    59	- {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.trimr`
    60	- {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.trimtail`
    61	- {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.trimboth`
    62	- {func}`scipy.stats.trimboth`
    63	- {func}`scipy.stats.trim1`
    64	
    65	So at the time of writing, SciPy offers at least half a dozen ways of taking a trimmed mean, but deciding which to use requires a lot of research, and none are able to perform *all* variations of the trimmed mean that we might reasonably expect them.
    66	
    67	What about other statistics - should we use {func}`scipy.stats.tvar`, {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.tvar`, or {func}`scipy.stats.mstats.trimmed_var` to compute the trimmed variance? If there are three functions for that and three for the mean, surely there is at least one function for computing the trimmed skewness? (Nope!)
    68	
    69	In the spirit of the Zen of Python, we suggest the new "one-- and preferably only one --obvious way" to calculate the trimmed version of many statistics: manually, in two or three lines of code, using more familiar, general-purpose features of NumPy and SciPy. Whether calculating a trimmed or winsorized statistic, there will be three steps:
    70	
    71	1. Identify the outlier threshold.
    72	2. Trim or winsorize.
    73	3. Compute the statistic.
    74	
    75	+++
    76	
    77	## Identifying the threshold
    78	
    79	SciPy now offers a {func}`quantile <scipy.stats.quantile>` function with several methods useful for identifying thresholds beyond which data are to be trimmed or winsorized. The most common thresholds are those corresponding with a certain *percentage* of the data in each tail. To set the threshold at $p=10\%$ of the data in each tail, rounding the number of points to trim *down*:
    80	
    81	```{code-cell} ipython3
    82	x = np.arange(22.)
    83	p = 0.1
    84	stats.quantile(x, [p, 1-p], method='round_outward')
    85	```
    86	
    87	Rounding the number of points to trim *up*:
    88	
    89	```{code-cell} ipython3
    90	stats.quantile(x, [p, 1-p], method='round_inward')
    91	```
    92	
    93	Rounding the number of points to trim to the nearest integer:
    94	
    95	```{code-cell} ipython3
    96	stats.quantile(x, [p, 1-p], method='round_nearest')
    97	```
    98	
    99	Note that these methods are all subtly different from estimating the 10th and 90th percentiles of the data, which might be called for in different circumstances. Using the default method:
   100	
   101	```{code-cell} ipython3
   102	stats.quantile(x, [p, 1-p])
   103	```
   104	
   105	The Harrell-Davis method:
   106	
   107	```{code-cell} ipython3
   108	stats.quantile(x, [p, 1-p], method='harrell-davis')
   109	```
   110	
   111	Note that {func}`quantile <scipy.stats.quantile>` is fully vectorized. Suppose we have two rows of data, and we wish to set different thresholds for each row.
   112	
   113	```{code-cell} ipython3
   114	x2d = np.stack([x, x])
   115	p2d = [[p  , 1 - p  ],  # thresholds for zeroth row
   116	       [2*p, 1 - 2*p]]  # thresholds for next row
   117	stats.quantile(x2d, p2d, method='round_outward', axis=-1)
   118	```
   119	
   120	### Missing data
   121	
   122	+++
   123	
   124	When there are missing or ignorable values but omitting them would lead to a ragged array, the two historical options have been to:
   125	
   126	- cover the missing/ignorable values with the "mask" of a NumPy masked array and use `stats.mstats` functions for analysis, or
   127	- replace missing/ignorable values with NaNs and use `stats` functions with `nan_policy='omit'` for analysis.
   128	
   129	The downsides of these approaches are documented elsewhere; here, we recommend using an [MArray](https://mdhaber.github.io/marray/tutorial.html) with `stats` functions.
   130	
   131	```{code-cell} ipython3
   132	from marray import numpy as mxp
   133	mask = ((x2d % 2) == 0)  # ignore odd elements
   134	y = mxp.asarray(x2d, mask=mask)
   135	print(y)
   136	```
   137	
   138	{func}`quantile <scipy.stats.quantile>` and many other `scipy.stats` functions natively support `MArray`s, and support grows with each version of SciPy.
   139	
   140	```{code-cell} ipython3
   141	stats.quantile(y, mxp.asarray(p2d), method='round_outward', axis=-1)
   142	```
   143	
   144	## Trimming and winsorizing
   145	
   146	+++
   147	
   148	### Winsorizing is `clip`ing
   149	
   150	+++
   151	
   152	Once a numerical threshold has been identified, winsorizing is simply an application of a fundamental data operation: `clip`.
   153	
   154	Suppose we have $n=22$ observations and we wish to winsorize $p=10\%$ from each side of the data. Rounding down with `method='round_outward'`, we would expect to winsorize $\left \lfloor{pn}\right \rfloor = 2$ points on each side. In this case, {func}`quantile <scipy.stats.quantile>` returns the *third* data point from the left (at index `2`) as the lower limit. `clip`ing to this limit will winsorize the two lower observations, leaving the third observation unchanged. The story is similar on the right.
